WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.200
This predator should've brought the plasma cannon.&nbsp;
And is that mask more style than substance?

00:00:05.877 --> 00:00:10.720
The particular Predator&nbsp;who arrives on Earth in "Prey" has a&nbsp;
ship that's shown a handful of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.200
mostly while it's transitioning in and out of&nbsp;
active camouflage. It's even visible as early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.840
as the first scene, where a slight disturbance&nbsp;
in the sky over Naru's village foreshadows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.560
alien's arrival. But what happens to the ship&nbsp;
after the Predator descends to the ground?

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.560
In the shot where the Predator is properly&nbsp;
deployed, he simply drops from his ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.160
which re-enters camouflage and disappears into a&nbsp;
nearby mass of clouds. Is the ship on autopilot?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.720
Are there other hunters onboard, setting off&nbsp;
for other spots on Earth or even other planets?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.840
What happens to it after Naru kills the Predator?&nbsp;
While these questions might all seem minor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.240
the grand scope of the story, their answers&nbsp;
would provide some interesting insight. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.760
unknown just how extensively the Predators&nbsp;
have explored Earth and humanity by 1719,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.160
and getting a look inside the ship would give&nbsp;
some clues. Perhaps for the best, though, "Prey"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.880
is more interested in the action on the ground,&nbsp;
leaving the many mysteries of the ship unanswered.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.240
Naru successfully uses the Predator's&nbsp;
helmet-mounted lock-on system to kill it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.440
but when she returns to her tribe&nbsp;
all she carries of the creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.320
is its severed head. What about the deployable&nbsp;
shield, the active camouflage, the armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.400
the weapons, and all the other pieces&nbsp;
of advanced technology? Was it damaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.680
beyond use in the fight? Is it impossible for&nbsp;
humans of the early 18th century to decipher?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.640
Or does Naru simply believe that she and her&nbsp;
people are better off without such strange tools?

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.880
That last option might be the most likely one.&nbsp;
"Prey" makes a point of showing how advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.320
technology can be an untrustworthy ally, such&nbsp;
as when the flintlock pistol given to Naru fails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.680
to fire, and when the Predator is killed by its&nbsp;
own weapon. Ultimately, instincts, intelligence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.200
and survival skills are shown to be far superior&nbsp;
to any piece of sci-fi tech, which might be why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.400
Naru lets the Predator's gear rot in the mud.&nbsp;
Or maybe, with the influx of European invaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.800
she'd simply rather leave the tools out of&nbsp;
potentially dangerous and treacherous hands.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.280
At the end of "Predator 2," Mike Harrigan&nbsp;
is given a gift by an elder Predator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.200
a flintlock pistol from the year 1715, as a sign&nbsp;
of respect. According to the name engraved on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.320
gun, it once belonged to Raphael Adolini. While&nbsp;
this moment is mainly meant to emphasize just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.200
how long the Predators have hunted on Earth, the&nbsp;
pistol has become a key piece of "Predator" lore.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.280
At the end of "Prey," it's revealed that&nbsp;
the flintlock pistol given to Naru midway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.280
through the movie is the same one seen in&nbsp;
"Predator 2." The character who gives her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.720
the gun is credited in the film as&nbsp;
"Raphael," which would seem to tie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.640
the whole thing up nicely. Or it would,&nbsp;
if not for a few lingering questions.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.120
The big mystery about the pistol now is how it&nbsp;
got back into the hands of the alien hunters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.520
Unless they specifically tracked it, which&nbsp;
seems unlikely, it would be incredibly unlikely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.400
for them to encounter whoever it got passed&nbsp;
down to decades or centuries later. The most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.200
logical explanation might be a pretty dark&nbsp;
one: The Predators return for Naru later on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.000
defeat her, and take the gun as a trophy.&nbsp;
Whatever the explanation, the return of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.800
flintlock pistol is a pretty notable retcon&nbsp;
of previously established "Predator" lore.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.360
Depending on what "Predator" stories you're&nbsp;
reading or watching, you might have one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.680
several different understandings of their&nbsp;
history on Earth. The "Alien vs. Predator"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.480
movies show a timeline where the species lived&nbsp;
among humans thousands of years in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.480
and the comics show Predators hunting humans as&nbsp;
early as 1718. That might sound like it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.880
line up perfectly with the events of "Prey,"&nbsp;
but the fifth film in the franchise actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.920
retcons some of the previous lore pretty severely&nbsp;
in terms of how long the hunters have been around.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.640
The comic book "Predator: 1718"&nbsp;
tells the story of Raphael Adolini,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.960
a pirate captain who encounters a Predator&nbsp;
shortly before his death. In the comic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.520
he gives his engraved flintlock pistol to&nbsp;
one of the aliens as a sign of respect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.040
It's the same gun that's given back to&nbsp;
humanity at the end of "Predator 2."

00:03:51.737 --> 00:03:53.111
"Take it."

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.760
But in "Prey," that flintlock pistol is given&nbsp;
to Naru by a member of the French trappers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.240
That would seem to undo the entire&nbsp;
"1718" story from any kind of canon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.880
which means that the actual history of&nbsp;
Predators on Earth is once again pretty murky.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.040
At the beginning of the movie, Naru delivers&nbsp;
a pretty interesting bit of narration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.240
explaining that long ago, a monster had come to&nbsp;
her land. Is she speaking about a time before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.600
events of "Prey," or after? Perhaps Predators have&nbsp;
come to her home before, but it's never confirmed.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:30.480
There are some pretty big differences between&nbsp;
the Predator in "Prey" and the ones seen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.640
the other films in the franchise. Primarily,&nbsp;
though the alien hunter still has a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.200
impressive technology, it's not nearly&nbsp;
to the level seen in later human hunts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.360
This is most visible in one particularly&nbsp;
glaring absence: the shoulder-mounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.240
plasma cannon that's typically been the&nbsp;
Predator's most iconic and dangerous weapon.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.560
In prior films, the plasma cannon is&nbsp;
connected to the triangular targeting laser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.080
used by the Predators to hunt down&nbsp;
their targets. In "Prey," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.960
that targeting apparatus is connected to a&nbsp;
handheld spear gun, still a deadly weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.920
as is proven multiple times, but not quite as&nbsp;
advanced as the plasma cannon. The simplest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.240
explanation for the classic weapon's absence&nbsp;
is that Predator technology just isn't quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.560
as advanced in "Prey," which would make sense&nbsp;
given that it's several hundred years removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:19.040
from the rest of the series. The Predator&nbsp;
also has a mechanical shield, rather than an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.160
energy-based one, and a much simpler helmet and&nbsp;
suit of armor than those worn by later hunters.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.640
Even still, it seems odd that a race that's&nbsp;
achieved invisibility and interstellar travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.680
hasn't yet mastered the science of big, deadly&nbsp;
laser guns. Another possible explanation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.840
that the hunter in "Prey" just chooses to use&nbsp;
simpler tools to make the hunt more challenging.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.600
Historically, Predators wear full helmets&nbsp;
that hide their mandibular alien faces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.480
In "Prey," however, the alien hunter that fights&nbsp;
Naru sports a very different piece of headgear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.080
More of a mask than a helmet, it looks&nbsp;
like it could be part of a skull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.400
possibly a trophy from a past hunt&nbsp;
modified with a laser-tracking system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.303
Why the Predator chooses to wear&nbsp;
the mask, however, remains unknown.

00:06:00.303 --> 00:06:05.015
"You're one ugly mother fu-"

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:10.960
At the very least, the mask suggests that&nbsp;
this isn't the Predator's first rodeo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.440
The hunter has killed before, but not necessarily&nbsp;
on Earth. With a spaceship at the ready,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.160
the mask could belong to any number of&nbsp;
as-yet-unnamed species across the galaxy.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.640
It's also unclear if using this kind of trophy&nbsp;
mask instead of the more traditional helmet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.120
is the norm of the time or a personal choice.&nbsp;
We don't get to see another Predator in "Prey,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.480
but the alien choosing the mask&nbsp;
instead of the classic armored helmet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.537
may be some kind of prideful display.

